# Composer
vendor/

# `composer.lock` IS tracked here, which is the opposite of what a library does and the right
# thing for this repository. It is a *measurement instrument*, not a build artifact: eleven of
# this project's dependencies are floating ranges (`spiral/roadrunner` — the whole RoadRunner
# arm — plus `spiral/goridge`, `nyholm/psr7`, `google/protobuf` and the `symfony` polyfills),
# so without a lock a `composer install` months later would benchmark a different RoadRunner
# against the same volucer and the comparison would be meaningless. The three `sailantis/*`
# packages are pinned by commit ref inside `composer.json` itself, so the lock adds nothing for
# them — but it is the only thing that fixes the other half. It is small (39 KB), text, and
# reviewable in a diff, which is exactly what a benchmark's inputs should be.
!composer.lock

# The seeded database is generated by `php seed.php`, not committed: it is 5 MB of
# benchmark rows, and a stale copy silently changes what every arm measures.
data/

# Harness output. A published run's raw per-repeat data is a deliverable and is
# committed (`results/raw.tsv`); everything else a run produces is per-host and
# per-run scratch.
#
# The crossover and latency passes are also named deliverables: they are the two
# passes that answer questions the sweep cannot, so their raw rows are kept alongside
# the sweep's rather than treated as scratch.
results/*.tsv
!results/raw.tsv
!results/crossover.tsv
!results/latency.tsv
# A superseded sweep is kept, not deleted: the report quotes ratios that only a
# later build can be compared against, and a comparison needs both sides.
!results/raw-*.tsv
# Prefork against one-process-per-slot: the evidence behind `spawn_cgi`'s `children` key,
# and the only measurement in this repository of what forked children save.
!results/prefork-vs-slots.tsv
# The matrix runs are deliverables for the same reason: each directory is one
# interleaved measurement of a grid, and the published benchmark pages are
# generated from these exact rows. `matrix85` is the server comparison, on
# PHP 8.5, every arm through one harness; `matrix85-root` is the same grid on
# the landing page; `matrix85-volucer-modes` is one server in five shapes;
# `matrix85-prefork` is the cold path against php-fpm at an equal number of
# serving processes. A published chart that cannot be traced to the rows
# behind it is a chart nobody can check.
!results/matrix85/
!results/matrix85-root/
!results/matrix85-volucer-modes/
!results/matrix85-prefork/
# The scale grid: the same eight arms at 16, 32 and 64 serving PHP processes, at
# c=8, 16, 32 and 64. `run85-scale-items` is the ORM page and `run85-scale-root`
# is the landing page - one run each, because the harness measures one endpoint
# per run. They are deliverables for the same reason as the rows above: the
# published pages are generated from these exact rows, and a chart that cannot
# be traced to the rows behind it is a chart nobody can check.
!results/run85-scale-items/
!results/run85-scale-root/
# The generated report tree is a build output, not a deliverable: it is
# reproduced by `scripts/bench-report.py` from the rows above, and the pages it
# produces are published into the volucer repository's documentation.
results/bench-report/
# The two interleaved A/B passes are named deliverables for the same reason the
# crossover and latency rows are: they answer questions the sweep cannot ask.
# `ab-perf.tsv` is the warm arm's throughput, measured in rotating order inside each
# round so that host drift charges every variant equally; `cpu-per-request.tsv` is the
# same three builds attributed per process, which is the measurement that localises a
# cost rather than merely detecting one. A superseded run of either is kept beside the
# current one the way a superseded sweep is.
!results/ab-perf.tsv
!results/ab-perf-*.tsv
!results/cpu-per-request.tsv
!results/cpu-per-request-*.tsv
# The slot-wait rows are a named deliverable too, and for the same reason: they are the
# pool's own account of how long requests waited for a slot, measured inside the server so
# that the client cannot be blamed for them. They are what turns "the tail is probably the
# queue" into an answer, and the report's table is derived from them. The `-ab-` pair is the
# same measurement interleaved — two builds inside one rotating round, RoadRunner as the
# control — which is the only layout that can settle the fair queue's throughput gain.
!results/slot-waits.tsv
!results/slot-waits-*.tsv
# The FrankenPHP arm's rows are a deliverable like the rest: they are the only measurement
# in this repository of a server that embeds PHP rather than spawning it, and the per-round
# spreads quoted in results/frankenphp.md are computed from them rather than re-measured.
!results/franken-ab.tsv
results/*.log
!results/run.log
*.log
# Un-ignored *last*, because a later line wins in gitignore: the interleaved A/B's console
# output, kept beside the rows it produced. It holds the per-cell pool lines and both reports,
# so the TSVs beside it are not a number without a provenance.
!results/slot-waits-fair-ab-run.log
!results/franken-ab-run.log

# The TLS-off A/B's rows are a deliverable like the rest: they are the only measurement in this
# repository of what the TLS work costs on a deployment that never negotiates it, and the server
# README's "what TLS costs when it is off" sentence cites this file. `-noise` is the A-vs-A
# control that sets the resolution the null result is bounded by, and it is kept for the same
# reason a negative control is kept: without it the null has no scale.
!results/tls-off-ab.tsv
!results/tls-off-ab-noise.tsv
# The reload-cost A/B is the same shape and a deliverable for the same reason: it is the only
# measurement of what the reload indirection costs on a request path that never reloads, and the
# server README's "what can be changed without a restart" section cites it. `-ab-20` is the run
# that supersedes the 10-round one, kept rather than overwritten so the ratio the report quotes
# can be re-derived from the repository; `-noise` is the A-vs-A control that gives the null its
# scale, which is what makes "no measurable difference" a statement rather than a hope.
!results/reload-cost-ab.tsv
!results/reload-cost-ab-20.tsv
!results/reload-cost-ab-noise.tsv
!results/reload-cost-ab-noise-20.tsv
# Editor / OS
.idea/
.vscode/
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# The Python report generator's bytecode cache. The generator is a
# measurement instrument like the shell scripts, and its cache is not part of
# any measurement.
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# The TLS-off A/B's harness is a Node script, because it was measured on a Windows host where
# `wrk` does not exist. Only the harness is here; its dependencies are not, for the same reason the
# `.sh` scripts do not commit `wrk` — `autocannon` is a load generator, not part of the experiment.
node_modules/
package-lock.json